1
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:16,000
What the hell?

2
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:17,890
Emergency preparedness drill.

3
00:00:17,890 --> 00:00:20,470
- Oh, no, come on! - Yeah, you know how it works.

4
00:00:20,770 --> 00:00:23,140
Once a quarter, keep our readiness up.

5
00:00:23,140 --> 00:00:24,890
Now, rise and shine, sleepy head.

6
00:00:24,890 --> 00:00:26,680
Half the town is probably dead.

7
00:00:28,440 --> 00:00:30,950
I have to get a lock for my door.

8
00:00:31,920 --> 00:00:33,670
I think you'll like the drill tonight.

9
00:00:33,670 --> 00:00:35,200
I've tried to make it fun.

10
00:00:35,200 --> 00:00:38,190
Each of these cards contains a detailed scenario

11
00:00:38,190 --> 00:00:40,130
of a possible apocalyptic event.

12
00:00:40,130 --> 00:00:42,030
Everything from wild fires

13
00:00:42,030 --> 00:00:44,420
to a surprise invasion by Canada.

14
00:00:46,060 --> 00:00:48,410
Pick a catastrophe, any catastrophe.

15
00:00:49,430 --> 00:00:53,800
Sheldon, Canada is not going to invade California.

16
00:00:54,290 --> 00:00:55,620
Yeah, really? You think those hippies

17
00:00:55,620 --> 00:00:57,460
in Washington and Oregon can stop them?

18
00:01:00,120 --> 00:01:01,340
Fine.

19
00:01:02,680 --> 00:01:03,450
All righty!

20
00:01:03,450 --> 00:01:07,480
"An 8.2 magnitude earthquake devastates Pasadena,

21
00:01:07,480 --> 00:01:09,750
reducing mighty edifices to dust,

22
00:01:09,750 --> 00:01:11,330
engulfing the city in flames.

23
00:01:11,330 --> 00:01:12,980
The streets flow with blood

24
00:01:12,980 --> 00:01:15,050
and echo with the cries of the wounded."

25
00:01:15,050 --> 00:01:16,490
Oh, excellent choice.

26
00:01:16,490 --> 00:01:19,090
Now, put on your hardhat and safety vest.

27
00:01:19,090 --> 00:01:20,310
Oh, fun.

28
00:01:20,800 --> 00:01:22,910
I get to spend another night in front of our apartment

29
00:01:22,910 --> 00:01:25,020
dressed like one of the Village People.

30
00:01:26,760 --> 00:01:28,270
You make that joke every three months.

31
00:01:28,270 --> 00:01:29,260
I still don't get it.

32
00:01:29,660 --> 00:01:31,280
Leonard... wait.

33
00:01:31,810 --> 00:01:32,560
What are you doing?

34
00:01:32,560 --> 00:01:34,490
I don't know, what am I doing?

35
00:01:35,120 --> 00:01:36,240
Look around you--

36
00:01:36,240 --> 00:01:38,800
there's hypothetical broken glass everywhere.

37
00:01:40,130 --> 00:01:43,360
Really? You're going to face Armageddon without your orthotics?

38
00:01:43,360 --> 00:01:44,490
Your choice.

39
00:01:46,500 --> 00:01:48,790
Uh-oh, hypothetical aftershock!

40
00:01:50,960 --> 00:01:52,860
And that's why we wear hardhats.

41
00:02:17,480 --> 00:02:20,310
Check it out-- press release from NASA.

42
00:02:20,680 --> 00:02:23,180
"Expedition 31 will launch this spring

43
00:02:23,180 --> 00:02:24,920
to the International Space Station.

44
00:02:24,920 --> 00:02:28,290
Crew members will include Commander Tom 'Tombo' Johnson,

45
00:02:28,290 --> 00:02:31,120
Astronaut Mike 'Supernova' Novacelik

46
00:02:31,370 --> 00:02:33,610
and Payload Specialist Howard Wolowitz."

47
00:02:34,880 --> 00:02:37,630
This is going right into my synagogue's newsletter.

48
00:02:39,770 --> 00:02:41,400
Dude, if you're going to be an astronaut,

49
00:02:41,400 --> 00:02:43,310
you need to pick a cool nickname.

50
00:02:43,310 --> 00:02:44,700
I don't get to pick it.

51
00:02:44,700 --> 00:02:46,030
The other guys have to give it to me.

52
00:02:46,030 --> 00:02:49,630
Oh. If I had one, it would be "Brown Dynamite."

53
00:02:51,080 --> 00:02:52,550
Are you not listening to me?

54
00:02:52,550 --> 00:02:55,560
The other astronauts have to give you your nickname.

55
00:02:55,560 --> 00:02:56,830
Are you not looking at me?

56
00:02:56,830 --> 00:02:58,720
I am Brown Dynamite.

57
00:03:02,290 --> 00:03:04,360
Why do you put six sugars in your coffee?

58
00:03:04,360 --> 00:03:05,800
Because the cafeteria doesn't offer

59
00:03:05,800 --> 00:03:08,070
little packets of methamphetamine.

60
00:03:09,660 --> 00:03:11,250
Emergency drill night last night, huh?

61
00:03:11,250 --> 00:03:12,630
- Uh-huh. - How'd you do?

62
00:03:12,630 --> 00:03:15,080
I'll tell you exactly how he did.

63
00:03:15,870 --> 00:03:17,630
"Readiness: unsatisfactory.

64
00:03:17,630 --> 00:03:19,910
Follows direction: barely.

65
00:03:19,910 --> 00:03:22,320
Attitude: a little too much.

66
00:03:23,310 --> 00:03:24,390
Overall:

67
00:03:24,390 --> 00:03:27,530
not only will he probably die in a fiery inferno,

68
00:03:27,530 --> 00:03:31,920
his incessant whining would most certainly spoil everyone else's day."

69
00:03:34,020 --> 00:03:35,990
You know what, I'm so tired, I can't even think straight.

70
00:03:35,990 --> 00:03:37,160
I'm going home.

71
00:03:37,160 --> 00:03:39,980
Will one of you guys give this nutbag a ride back later?

72
00:03:40,530 --> 00:03:41,660
You can't go home.

73
00:03:41,660 --> 00:03:43,800
You have to take me to the dentist at 4:00.

74
00:03:43,800 --> 00:03:46,130
Oh... can't you take the bus to the dentist?

75
00:03:46,130 --> 00:03:48,050
Of course I can. It's coming back,

76
00:03:48,050 --> 00:03:50,010
under the residual effects of the anesthesia,

77
00:03:50,010 --> 00:03:50,910
that's the problem.

78
00:03:51,050 --> 00:03:52,070
Two years ago,

79
00:03:52,070 --> 00:03:54,890
after a deep gum cleaning, I thought I got on a bus

80
00:03:54,890 --> 00:03:57,780
but somehow wound up on a booze cruise to Mexico.

81
00:04:00,710 --> 00:04:02,470
They put you under for a cleaning?

82
00:04:02,470 --> 00:04:04,400
Yeah, they have to-- I'm a biter.

83
00:04:06,080 --> 00:04:07,970
Whatever, Sheldon. I'm exhausted.

84
00:04:07,970 --> 00:04:09,490
I'm not taking you to the dentist.

85
00:04:09,490 --> 00:04:10,510
Wrong, sir.

86
00:04:10,510 --> 00:04:13,870
Wrong. Under Section 37-B of the Roommate Agreement--

87
00:04:13,870 --> 00:04:16,160
"Miscellaneous Duties"-- you are obligated

88
00:04:16,160 --> 00:04:17,310
to take me to the dentist.

89
00:04:17,310 --> 00:04:20,610
See? It's right here after "providing a confirmation sniff

90
00:04:20,610 --> 00:04:22,610
on questionable dairy products."

91
00:04:24,260 --> 00:04:27,360
You know what, I am sick of the Roommate Agreement.

92
00:04:29,710 --> 00:04:31,060
It's ridiculous.

93
00:04:31,060 --> 00:04:33,250
I'm your roommate, not your chauffeur.

94
00:04:33,250 --> 00:04:34,810
You know, I had better things to do yesterday

95
00:04:34,810 --> 00:04:37,640
than drive you all the way to the "good" model train store

96
00:04:37,640 --> 00:04:39,700
in Garden Grove because the one in Pasadena

97
00:04:39,700 --> 00:04:42,340
has gotten "too big for its britches."

98
00:04:43,120 --> 00:04:44,180
Well, it has.

99
00:04:44,180 --> 00:04:45,820
Ask anybody.

100
00:04:46,420 --> 00:04:48,780
I don't care. I'm done.

101
00:04:48,860 --> 00:04:49,860
Hold on.

102
00:04:50,330 --> 00:04:53,290
Are you saying that you want to invoke Clause 209?

103
00:04:53,290 --> 00:04:54,330
I don't know what that is,

104
00:04:54,330 --> 00:04:56,680
but if it means I can go home and sleep, then yes.

105
00:04:56,680 --> 00:04:58,010
Think carefully here.

106
00:04:58,010 --> 00:05:00,490
Clause 209 suspends our friendship,

107
00:05:00,490 --> 00:05:03,290
and strips down the Roommate Agreement to its bare essentials.

108
00:05:03,290 --> 00:05:07,120
Our responsibilities toward each other would only be rent, utilities

109
00:05:07,120 --> 00:05:09,070
and a perfunctory chin jut of recognition

110
00:05:09,070 --> 00:05:10,250
as we pass in the hall.

111
00:05:10,250 --> 00:05:11,350
'Sup?

112
00:05:15,520 --> 00:05:16,880
Where do I sign?

113
00:05:18,940 --> 00:05:20,040
Right here.

114
00:05:20,410 --> 00:05:21,650
Use your finger.

115
00:05:22,500 --> 00:05:25,590
- There. Done. - All right. That's it.

116
00:05:25,590 --> 00:05:27,460
We are now no longer companions,

117
00:05:27,460 --> 00:05:28,860
boon or otherwise.

118
00:05:28,860 --> 00:05:30,870
We are not merely acquaintances.

119
00:05:30,870 --> 00:05:32,720
To amend the words of Toy Story--

120
00:05:32,720 --> 00:05:34,540
"You have not got a friend in me."

121
00:05:36,550 --> 00:05:38,150
I'm gonna go home and take a nap.

122
00:05:38,150 --> 00:05:39,840
Yeah, well, tell it to someone who cares.

123
00:05:42,480 --> 00:05:44,360
I got pretty exciting NASA news today.

124
00:05:44,360 --> 00:05:46,000
Next week I fly to Houston

125
00:05:46,000 --> 00:05:49,710
for orientation and zero-gravity elimination drills.

126
00:05:50,740 --> 00:05:51,660
What does that mean?

127
00:05:51,660 --> 00:05:53,940
He's gonna learn to poop in space.

128
00:05:56,570 --> 00:05:58,750
Open the pod bay doors, HAL.

129
00:06:00,430 --> 00:06:03,310
Maybe your nickname should be "Brown Dynamite."

130
00:06:06,670 --> 00:06:08,670
Hello, dear friends.

131
00:06:08,670 --> 00:06:10,320
And Dr. Hofstadter.

132
00:06:11,500 --> 00:06:12,730
'Sup?

133
00:06:14,340 --> 00:06:15,350
'Sup?

134
00:06:16,080 --> 00:06:17,230
My apologies.

135
00:06:17,230 --> 00:06:18,400
I would've been here sooner,

136
00:06:18,400 --> 00:06:21,500
but the bus kept stopping for other people to get on it.

137
00:06:22,930 --> 00:06:24,620
I saved you a dumpling.

138
00:06:24,700 --> 00:06:26,650
Oh, your concern for me is touching.

139
00:06:26,650 --> 00:06:27,810
It will serve you well

140
00:06:27,810 --> 00:06:29,820
when you take me to the dentist tomorrow.

141
00:06:30,700 --> 00:06:32,530
I'm sorry, Sheldon, I'm busy.

142
00:06:32,530 --> 00:06:34,350
I'm right in the middle of my addiction study.

143
00:06:34,350 --> 00:06:36,180
I've got a lab full of alcoholic monkeys,

144
00:06:36,180 --> 00:06:38,340
and tomorrow's the day we switch them to O'Doul's.

145
00:06:40,050 --> 00:06:41,090
You're my girlfriend

146
00:06:41,090 --> 00:06:43,210
and you're not going to cater to my every need?

147
00:06:43,210 --> 00:06:45,790
Oh, where'd the magic go?

148
00:06:47,000 --> 00:06:49,460
Sheldon, that's not what girlfriends are for.

149
00:06:49,520 --> 00:06:51,160
Although, you don't use them for what they're for,

150
00:06:51,160 --> 00:06:52,860
so what do I know?

151
00:06:59,960 --> 00:07:01,650
Howard doesn't make me do his shopping

152
00:07:01,650 --> 00:07:02,930
or take him to the dentist

153
00:07:02,930 --> 00:07:04,540
or pick up his dry cleaning, right?

154
00:07:04,540 --> 00:07:05,690
Absolutely.

155
00:07:05,690 --> 00:07:08,690
But when Ma's hips give out, you're up, kid.

156
00:07:10,200 --> 00:07:13,110
Well, if Amy's too busy, that gives the rest of you

157
00:07:13,110 --> 00:07:15,180
an opportunity to make my life easier,

158
00:07:15,180 --> 00:07:18,220
thus assuring yourselves a footnote in my memoirs,

159
00:07:18,220 --> 00:07:21,340
tentatively entitled You're Welcome, Mankind.

160
00:07:21,610 --> 00:07:22,780
All right, then,

161
00:07:22,780 --> 00:07:25,480
just shout when you hear the task you want to undertake.

162
00:07:25,480 --> 00:07:27,270
Uh, dentist.

163
00:07:31,360 --> 00:07:33,380
Okay, w-we can circle back to that one.

164
00:07:33,580 --> 00:07:36,020
Um, well, who wants to take me Wednesday morning

165
00:07:36,020 --> 00:07:38,700
to get new heels put on my dress shoes?

166
00:07:42,400 --> 00:07:43,140
Anyone?

167
00:07:43,240 --> 00:07:45,910
Oh. That one had hoot written all over it.

168
00:07:45,910 --> 00:07:48,810
Um, all right, uh, dermatologist.

169
00:07:51,710 --> 00:07:52,970
Allergist.

170
00:07:54,630 --> 00:07:56,170
Podiatrist.

171
00:07:57,520 --> 00:07:59,340
Supercuts?

172
00:08:00,010 --> 00:08:01,610
Okay, okay, here's a fun one.

173
00:08:01,610 --> 00:08:03,780
Um, I need a new picture frame

174
00:08:03,780 --> 00:08:06,920
and I have a hankering for Swedish meatballs.

175
00:08:06,930 --> 00:08:09,320
Who wants to spend the day with me at IKEA?

176
00:08:11,560 --> 00:08:13,300
- Their meatballs are pretty good.  - What's that?

177
00:08:13,300 --> 00:08:14,550
Nothing, nothing.

178
00:08:17,780 --> 00:08:19,070
Hello, Stuart.

179
00:08:19,100 --> 00:08:20,430
Oh, hey, Sheldon.

180
00:08:20,430 --> 00:08:22,180
Can I help you find something today?

181
00:08:22,340 --> 00:08:23,410
No, no.

182
00:08:23,410 --> 00:08:24,860
I was just sitting at home

183
00:08:24,860 --> 00:08:26,760
thinking about how it might be nice to catch up

184
00:08:26,760 --> 00:08:28,710
with my ninth favorite person.

185
00:08:29,550 --> 00:08:30,440
Ninth?

186
00:08:30,440 --> 00:08:31,880
You moved up one.

187
00:08:32,780 --> 00:08:35,680
My pen pal in Somalia was kidnapped by pirates.

188
00:08:37,740 --> 00:08:39,670
So, uh... how are you?

189
00:08:39,670 --> 00:08:41,300
Uh... not so good.

190
00:08:41,300 --> 00:08:44,630
My shrink just killed himself and...

191
00:08:44,630 --> 00:08:46,760
blamed me in the note.

192
00:08:49,110 --> 00:08:50,640
Great. Great.

193
00:08:52,640 --> 00:08:54,680
So, what's new with your family?

194
00:08:54,680 --> 00:08:57,020
How's your mother? Is she alive?

195
00:08:57,020 --> 00:08:58,180
Yeah.

196
00:08:58,180 --> 00:09:00,680
And your father? Alive?

197
00:09:01,000 --> 00:09:01,990
Yes.

198
00:09:02,090 --> 00:09:04,230
How about your grandparents, they alive?

199
00:09:04,830 --> 00:09:05,680
No.

200
00:09:05,680 --> 00:09:07,500
Oh, I'm so sorry for your loss.

201
00:09:08,330 --> 00:09:09,780
On a cheerier note,

202
00:09:09,780 --> 00:09:12,950
I have a teeth-cleaning appointment this afternoon.

203
00:09:13,050 --> 00:09:16,060
What do you say you take me over there, buddy boy?

204
00:09:17,680 --> 00:09:19,870
I'm sorry, you want me to take you to the dentist?

205
00:09:19,870 --> 00:09:22,560
Yes. Now, I can't make any promises,

206
00:09:22,560 --> 00:09:25,720
but that's the sort of thing that gets a fella on the short list

207
00:09:25,720 --> 00:09:27,610
for the number eight friend slot.

208
00:09:28,870 --> 00:09:29,810
Sheldon, I'm working.

209
00:09:29,810 --> 00:09:31,520
I can't take you to the dentist.

210
00:09:31,520 --> 00:09:34,250
Also-- and I can't stress this enough--

211
00:09:34,250 --> 00:09:37,040
I don't want to take you to the dentist.

212
00:09:38,130 --> 00:09:40,820
Can't help a friend out in a time of need, huh?

213
00:09:40,820 --> 00:09:42,980
I see where your therapist was coming from.

214
00:09:47,740 --> 00:09:51,010
Ooh, ooh! What about we make your astronaut nickname

215
00:09:51,010 --> 00:09:53,750
Howard "Buzz" Wolowitz?

216
00:09:55,130 --> 00:09:56,750
You can't do "Buzz."

217
00:09:56,750 --> 00:09:58,280
"Buzz" is taken.

218
00:09:58,280 --> 00:10:00,220
Buzz Lightyear is not real.

219
00:10:03,920 --> 00:10:06,440
No, that's not what I'm talking about.

220
00:10:06,440 --> 00:10:09,390
Well, are you talking about when he thought he was real?

221
00:10:12,210 --> 00:10:13,350
No.

222
00:10:14,740 --> 00:10:16,670
Okay, um... how about "Crash"?

223
00:10:16,670 --> 00:10:18,810
Howard "Crash" Wolowitz.

224
00:10:20,010 --> 00:10:21,380
Yeah, terrific. The other astronauts

225
00:10:21,380 --> 00:10:23,350
would love to go hurtling through space

226
00:10:23,350 --> 00:10:25,080
with a guy named "Crash."

227
00:10:26,480 --> 00:10:27,750
All right, um, how about...

228
00:10:27,750 --> 00:10:30,060
Oh, how about "Rocket Man"?

229
00:10:31,930 --> 00:10:34,560
That's not bad, Howard "Rocket Man" Wolowitz.

230
00:10:34,560 --> 00:10:35,800
Yeah, it's great, but I told you,

231
00:10:35,800 --> 00:10:37,530
I don't get to pick my nickname.

232
00:10:37,530 --> 00:10:39,420
It has to come from the other astronauts.

233
00:10:39,550 --> 00:10:42,170
Maybe there's a way to get them to come up with it.

234
00:10:42,170 --> 00:10:43,590
Like how?

235
00:10:43,590 --> 00:10:45,850
Once I tried carrying around a Duncan yo-yo,

236
00:10:45,850 --> 00:10:48,530
hoping the other kids would start calling me Duncan.

237
00:10:50,140 --> 00:10:51,090
Did it work?

238
00:10:51,090 --> 00:10:53,780
No, they ended up calling me Sock Mouth.

239
00:10:55,030 --> 00:10:56,480
Because they took away my yo-yo

240
00:10:56,480 --> 00:10:58,270
and stuffed their socks in my mouth.

241
00:11:01,040 --> 00:11:05,400
Okay, uh, what if we make "Rocket Man" your ringtone,

242
00:11:05,400 --> 00:11:06,900
and the next time you talk to those guys,

243
00:11:06,900 --> 00:11:08,460
I'll call you and they'll hear it.

244
00:11:08,460 --> 00:11:09,810
Plant the seed.

245
00:11:10,620 --> 00:11:12,050
That's actually not a terrible plan.

246
00:11:12,390 --> 00:11:14,520
They don't call me "Brown Dynamite" for nothing.

247
00:11:18,130 --> 00:11:19,070
'Sup?

248
00:11:19,070 --> 00:11:19,720
'Sup?

249
00:11:21,690 --> 00:11:22,940
Hey, did you ever make it to the dentist?

250
00:11:22,940 --> 00:11:24,240
Not necessary. No.

251
00:11:24,240 --> 00:11:27,040
I found a service that'll send a van to your house

252
00:11:27,040 --> 00:11:28,190
for a teeth cleaning.

253
00:11:28,190 --> 00:11:30,420
Mostly they cater to dogs, but...

254
00:11:32,650 --> 00:11:34,990
...the fellow on the phone seemed to have an open mind.

255
00:11:35,950 --> 00:11:37,010
All right, Sheldon,

256
00:11:37,010 --> 00:11:38,420
if you need me to take you to the dentist,

257
00:11:38,420 --> 00:11:40,310
I will take you to the dentist.

258
00:11:40,440 --> 00:11:42,890
Are you suggesting that you've come to your senses

259
00:11:42,890 --> 00:11:45,580
and wish to reestablish the mutual benefits that stem

260
00:11:45,580 --> 00:11:47,940
from full participation in the Roommate Agreement?

261
00:11:47,940 --> 00:11:48,910
Absolutely.

262
00:11:48,910 --> 00:11:50,470
If you admit that you're a 30-year-old man

263
00:11:50,470 --> 00:11:52,440
who's incapable of functioning on his own.

264
00:11:53,380 --> 00:11:55,910
Ooh, Sock Mouth's got him on the ropes.

265
00:11:57,390 --> 00:11:59,050
I will admit nothing of the sort.

266
00:11:59,050 --> 00:12:00,540
And now if you'll excuse me,

267
00:12:00,540 --> 00:12:02,480
I have to call my dentist and see

268
00:12:02,480 --> 00:12:05,240
if I can also get my hair shampooed and my nails clipped.

269
00:12:17,180 --> 00:12:19,050
Oh, good, your power's out, too.

270
00:12:19,050 --> 00:12:20,270
Why is that good?

271
00:12:20,730 --> 00:12:21,590
Because last month,

272
00:12:21,590 --> 00:12:23,980
I sent the electric company a Starbucks gift card,

273
00:12:23,980 --> 00:12:27,270
an apology note, and a few snapshots of me in a bra.

274
00:12:28,890 --> 00:12:29,700
Power failure.

275
00:12:29,700 --> 00:12:31,910
Implementing power-failure protocol.

276
00:12:33,280 --> 00:12:35,600
What happened to all your glow- in-the-dark-emergency-exit stuff

277
00:12:35,600 --> 00:12:37,320
you had painted on the floor?

278
00:12:37,320 --> 00:12:39,500
Oh, that was wildly carcinogenic.

279
00:12:39,920 --> 00:12:41,110
Anyway...

280
00:12:41,110 --> 00:12:42,660
it's too bad you're no longer entitled

281
00:12:42,660 --> 00:12:44,650
to the full benefits of my friendship,

282
00:12:44,650 --> 00:12:47,160
because I happen to be extremely prepared

283
00:12:47,160 --> 00:12:48,400
for such an emergency.

284
00:12:48,400 --> 00:12:51,140
Please try not to see anything by this light.

285
00:12:51,650 --> 00:12:53,250
It's not for you.

286
00:12:54,410 --> 00:12:57,200
It's just a blackout; I'm sure the power will be back on soon.

287
00:12:57,200 --> 00:12:59,420
And I'm sure some fool in the Donner party

288
00:12:59,420 --> 00:13:01,540
said the snow would stop any day now.

289
00:13:03,390 --> 00:13:05,520
I like to think they ate him first.

290
00:13:06,290 --> 00:13:08,280
You know, I got some candles in my apartment.

291
00:13:08,280 --> 00:13:10,000
But candles?! During a blackout?!

292
00:13:10,000 --> 00:13:11,750
Are you mad? That's a fire hazard.

293
00:13:11,750 --> 00:13:13,800
No, Pasadena Water and Power recommends

294
00:13:13,800 --> 00:13:16,140
the far safer glow stick.

295
00:13:17,570 --> 00:13:19,240
You call that a glow stick?

296
00:13:24,920 --> 00:13:26,970
That is a glow stick.

297
00:13:27,780 --> 00:13:28,940
Come on, let's go.

298
00:13:28,940 --> 00:13:30,880
Before you go, consider this.

299
00:13:30,880 --> 00:13:34,420
Not only do I have a deep-cycle marine battery power source

300
00:13:34,420 --> 00:13:35,650
which is more than capable

301
00:13:35,650 --> 00:13:36,990
of running our entertainment system,

302
00:13:36,990 --> 00:13:38,220
I also have

303
00:13:38,220 --> 00:13:41,730
all 61 episodes of the BBC series Red Dwarf...

304
00:13:42,300 --> 00:13:44,810
and Fiddle-Faddle.

305
00:13:45,600 --> 00:13:46,660
All yours

306
00:13:46,660 --> 00:13:49,410
if you're willing to reinstate the Roommate Agreement.

307
00:13:50,010 --> 00:13:53,060
I've got wine at my place and some bubble wrap we could pop.

308
00:13:58,240 --> 00:13:59,390
Oh, he'll be back.

309
00:13:59,390 --> 00:14:00,980
Wine and a girl in the dark--

310
00:14:00,980 --> 00:14:02,830
he's gonna be bored out of his mind.

311
00:14:05,370 --> 00:14:07,110
To wine and bubble wrap.

312
00:14:09,230 --> 00:14:11,620
And to not having to watch Sheldon demonstrate

313
00:14:11,620 --> 00:14:13,170
his reverse osmosis machine

314
00:14:13,170 --> 00:14:15,950
that converts urine into drinking water.

315
00:14:17,610 --> 00:14:19,760
You know, in Girl Scouts, Tammie Dineesha said

316
00:14:19,760 --> 00:14:21,600
you could do that with panty hose.

317
00:14:22,470 --> 00:14:23,820
Boy, was she wrong.

318
00:14:26,360 --> 00:14:28,210
Anyway, you want to make out?

319
00:14:29,250 --> 00:14:31,610
I thought because our relationship's in a beta test,

320
00:14:31,610 --> 00:14:33,650
you wanted to take things slow.

321
00:14:33,650 --> 00:14:34,560
Okay.

322
00:14:34,560 --> 00:14:36,170
Do you want to make out slow?

323
00:14:38,520 --> 00:14:42,700
I can go so slow it'll be like there's a snail in your mouth.

324
00:14:48,220 --> 00:14:50,740
Well, lucky for you, there's nothing else to do right now.

325
00:14:53,640 --> 00:14:54,470
Excuse me, Leonard.

326
00:14:54,470 --> 00:14:56,020
Since when don't you knock?

327
00:14:56,020 --> 00:14:57,930
It's like the only good thing about you.

328
00:14:59,140 --> 00:15:01,150
Social niceties have been suspended, Leonard.

329
00:15:01,150 --> 00:15:02,890
We're in a state of emergency.

330
00:15:02,980 --> 00:15:05,770
The world has descended into darkened turmoil.

331
00:15:05,770 --> 00:15:08,440
Lawlessness and savagery are the order of the day.

332
00:15:10,070 --> 00:15:11,050
Fine, what is it?

333
00:15:11,050 --> 00:15:12,690
I'm making s'mores.

334
00:15:13,370 --> 00:15:14,480
I wanted to alert you

335
00:15:14,480 --> 00:15:16,620
in case you smelled caramelizing marshmallows

336
00:15:16,620 --> 00:15:19,330
and thought a nearby candy factory was on fire.

337
00:15:19,700 --> 00:15:21,350
S'mores, huh? Good for you.

338
00:15:21,350 --> 00:15:24,100
Yes, or good for us

339
00:15:24,100 --> 00:15:25,550
if you sign here

340
00:15:25,550 --> 00:15:27,680
and reinstate the full Roommate Agreement.

341
00:15:27,680 --> 00:15:29,080
No, thanks. I'm good.

342
00:15:29,080 --> 00:15:30,100
Really?

343
00:15:30,100 --> 00:15:31,790
Huh! Okay.

344
00:15:31,790 --> 00:15:34,520
In that case, I will have a s'more by myself.

345
00:15:34,870 --> 00:15:37,500
And then I'm gonna have s'more.

346
00:15:41,620 --> 00:15:43,050
By myself.

347
00:15:44,150 --> 00:15:45,750
No, don't "aw" him.

348
00:15:45,750 --> 00:15:47,280
He brought this all on himself.

349
00:15:47,280 --> 00:15:48,330
But he's sad.

350
00:15:48,330 --> 00:15:49,590
No, he's crazy.

351
00:15:49,590 --> 00:15:51,120
Sometimes crazy looks like sad

352
00:15:51,120 --> 00:15:52,920
so it'll suck you back in.

353
00:15:55,550 --> 00:15:57,930
I think he misses his little buddy.

354
00:15:59,470 --> 00:16:00,580
Fine.

355
00:16:01,320 --> 00:16:02,530
But mark my words,

356
00:16:02,530 --> 00:16:06,840
this frustrating, bogus teenage make-out session is not over.

357
00:16:16,510 --> 00:16:18,250
I thought you said candles were dangerous.

358
00:16:18,250 --> 00:16:20,140
This is a Bunsen burner.

359
00:16:21,770 --> 00:16:24,440
I'm a scientist; I know what I'm doing.

360
00:16:25,550 --> 00:16:26,890
Oh, drat.

361
00:16:33,300 --> 00:16:36,160
It took me a gallon of urine to make that water.

362
00:16:40,250 --> 00:16:41,820
Listen, Sheldon, this is stupid.

363
00:16:41,820 --> 00:16:43,630
I don't see why we can't be friends.

364
00:16:43,630 --> 00:16:45,090
And I'm willing to drive you around

365
00:16:45,090 --> 00:16:46,300
and help you out with stuff.

366
00:16:46,300 --> 00:16:47,240
I just don't want to do it

367
00:16:47,240 --> 00:16:49,350
because of some silly Roommate Agreement.

368
00:16:49,650 --> 00:16:50,890
What are you proposing?

369
00:16:50,890 --> 00:16:53,030
That we go back to the way things were.

370
00:16:53,490 --> 00:16:54,870
But when I do something for you,

371
00:16:54,870 --> 00:16:56,830
you show a little appreciation.

372
00:16:57,190 --> 00:16:58,850
And how would I do that?

373
00:17:00,620 --> 00:17:02,040
You say "thank you."

374
00:17:02,040 --> 00:17:03,710
Every time?

375
00:17:05,940 --> 00:17:07,780
It's not crazy.

376
00:17:08,520 --> 00:17:10,010
Counter-proposal.

377
00:17:10,010 --> 00:17:12,370
We reinstate the full Roommate Agreement

378
00:17:12,370 --> 00:17:13,930
with the following addendum:

379
00:17:13,930 --> 00:17:17,650
in the spirit of Mother's Day or Father's Day,

380
00:17:17,650 --> 00:17:19,790
once a year, we set aside a day

381
00:17:19,790 --> 00:17:23,010
to celebrate all your contributions to my life,

382
00:17:23,010 --> 00:17:26,120
both actual and imagined by you.

383
00:17:27,540 --> 00:17:29,810
We could call it Leonard's Day.

384
00:17:31,130 --> 00:17:33,110
I kind of like the sound of that.

385
00:17:33,580 --> 00:17:34,420
Of course you do.

386
00:17:34,420 --> 00:17:36,630
It's about you, like everything else.

387
00:17:36,630 --> 00:17:38,960
Oh, thank goodness.

388
00:17:38,960 --> 00:17:41,740
I don't think I had it in me to make another glass of water.

389
00:17:42,650 --> 00:17:45,610
- So, do I get breakfast in bed on Leonard's Day? - No.

390
00:17:46,100 --> 00:17:48,680
- Can I sit in your spot? - No.

391
00:17:48,680 --> 00:17:50,220
Can I control the thermostat? No.

392
00:17:51,210 --> 00:17:52,110
Do I get a card?

393
00:17:52,110 --> 00:17:53,940
Of course you get a card.

394
00:17:55,000 --> 00:17:56,600
It's Leonard's Day.

395
00:17:57,840 --> 00:17:59,580
Hey, guys, the building manager said

396
00:17:59,580 --> 00:18:00,840
the reason the power went out is

397
00:18:00,840 --> 00:18:02,030
someone went down into the basement

398
00:18:02,030 --> 00:18:04,130
and just pulled the main breaker switch.

399
00:18:07,880 --> 00:18:09,440
Really.

400
00:18:10,440 --> 00:18:12,240
Who do you think did that, Sheldon?

401
00:18:12,870 --> 00:18:15,780
Oh, I don't know.

402
00:18:18,270 --> 00:18:20,460
But whoever that mystery man was,

403
00:18:20,460 --> 00:18:22,080
you should be eternally grateful,

404
00:18:22,080 --> 00:18:24,650
for without him, there would be no Leonard's Day.

405
00:18:26,420 --> 00:18:27,700
Leonard's Day?

406
00:18:27,700 --> 00:18:28,040
Oh, no pressure.

407
00:18:28,040 --> 00:18:30,000
Just get him a crummy card, you're good.

408
00:18:34,460 --> 00:18:36,770
All right, test my ring tone.

409
00:18:42,260 --> 00:18:44,340
- That really is a good song. - Oh, yeah.

410
00:18:44,340 --> 00:18:46,140
There's a reason he's Sir Elton John.

411
00:18:46,140 --> 00:18:47,270
They don't make you a knight

412
00:18:47,270 --> 00:18:49,580
for writing "Wake Me Up Before You Go Go."

413
00:18:51,200 --> 00:18:53,850
Howard, are you coming down for breakfast?!

414
00:18:53,850 --> 00:18:56,890
Ma, I told you I have a video conference with NASA!

415
00:18:56,890 --> 00:18:58,280
I said don't bother me!

416
00:18:58,280 --> 00:19:01,010
Oh! Listen to Mr. Big Shot Astronaut!

417
00:19:01,020 --> 00:19:05,070
Yes, please listen to Mr. Big Shot Astronaut!

418
00:19:10,010 --> 00:19:11,590
Hey, good morning.

419
00:19:11,590 --> 00:19:14,410
Hey, Howard, thanks for getting up so early.

420
00:19:14,410 --> 00:19:16,410
No problem, Dr. Massimino.

421
00:19:16,800 --> 00:19:18,450
The guys here call me Mass.

422
00:19:18,450 --> 00:19:20,470
Mass! That's a cool nickname.

423
00:19:20,470 --> 00:19:23,450
'Cause force equals mass times acceleration.

424
00:19:23,450 --> 00:19:24,550
Yeah.

425
00:19:24,550 --> 00:19:26,580
It's just short for Massimino.

426
00:19:29,180 --> 00:19:32,650
Anyway, the plan for this morning is to go over the...

427
00:19:32,650 --> 00:19:34,710
Sorry. My phone.

428
00:19:35,610 --> 00:19:37,750
What is that? Is that "Rocket Man"?

429
00:19:37,750 --> 00:19:39,220
Yeah, my ring tone.

430
00:19:39,230 --> 00:19:42,350
Kind of my favorite song, "Rocket Man."

431
00:19:42,350 --> 00:19:43,160
Howard,

432
00:19:43,160 --> 00:19:45,980
your Fruit Loops are getting soggy!

433
00:19:48,320 --> 00:19:49,700
Not now!

434
00:19:49,700 --> 00:19:50,840
Who's that?

435
00:19:50,840 --> 00:19:53,000
My mom. Sorry.

436
00:19:53,000 --> 00:19:56,250
No problem... Fruit Loops.

